// The UNVERSIONED `namespace std` under libc++: <new> declares its
// ABI-stable entry points in a `namespace std // purposefully not using
// versioning namespace` block, so they mangle _ZSt... in BOTH flavors —
// std::get_new_handler() is _ZSt15get_new_handlerv, never
// _ZNSt3__115get_new_handlerEv (which libc++.so does not export; the old
// blanket std->std::__1 widening minted exactly that undefined import and
// the run died at MIR link). The declared scope decides the mangling; the
// .flags fixture pins this test to -stdlib=libc++ where the split is
// observable (under libstdc++ std entities are directly in std either way).
// The gate asserts the import RESOLVES and the call runs — a wrong scope is
// an undefined MIR import and "ok" never prints.
// (new_handler VALUE handling is deliberately not gated here: the typedef'd
// function-pointer return/param family has its own pre-existing gaps —
// task #91: the param mangles as a source name, and the returned pointer
// mis-compares against 0.)
// Oracle: clang++ -stdlib=libc++ runs it and prints "ok".
#include <new>
#include <cstdio>

int main()
{
    std::get_new_handler();
    printf("ok\n");
    return 0;
}
